Actually, that's a (minor) argument in the other direction:  one
would expect everything from a bones pile to *not* have been
worn (by the current player), so any "worn" state that they
had in the other game would need to be cleared, either when
the bones are saved or when they are loaded, if the state is
stored in the object struct.  If it's a separate list elsewhere in
the game state, not connected to the object struct itself or to
any particular level, this would happen for free by default.

Although, if what you're tracking is smallness rather than
having been worn by the current player, then the argument
swings back the other way again.


